import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'1.1'))
from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('5.1')
for local,texts in {
'01':['Profit maximisation means choosing the feasible output or method with the greatest difference between total revenue and total cost, rather than merely increasing profit.','Higher revenue or output does not establish higher profit without the associated costs.','The marginal condition for a profit maximum is marginal revenue equal to marginal cost with the relevant crossing or maximum condition; a discrete schedule can instead be compared using total profits.'],
'02':['Production is the creation of goods and services to satisfy wants.','Providing a service is production even where no tangible object is manufactured.'],
'03':['Demand for factors is derived from demand for the output they help produce.','Factor demand is influenced by factor productivity, factor prices and the availability and cost of substitutes or complements.'],
'04':['Substitution changes the combination of factors used, such as replacing some labour tasks with capital equipment.','A lower-cost method raises profit at unchanged revenue only when all relevant costs, output quality and feasibility are included.','Capital can replace some tasks while complementing other labour, so substitution need not eliminate every job.']}.items():
 for text in texts:t.claim(local,text)
t.block('01','plain_explanation','Profit is a difference, and maximisation is a comparison',"""
A private firm may aim to maximise profit: obtain the largest possible difference between total revenue and total cost from its feasible choices. Profit is not the same as revenue, and maximum profit is not the same as maximum output. More sales can bring additional receipts while adding even more cost. A method that increases profit from its old level may still fall short of the best available alternative.

Use invented weekly figures for Kalahari Clay Works, with all relevant costs included and each output sold. At 10 batches total revenue is N$1,000 and total cost N$800; at 20 batches the figures are N$1,800 and N$1,300; at 30 batches they are N$2,400 and N$2,000. Profits are N$200, N$500 and N$400. The 20-batch option maximises profit among these supplied choices. The 30-batch option has the highest revenue and output but less profit. Unless all feasible choices are represented, describe the conclusion as the best of those listed.

In marginal terms, marginal revenue is additional revenue from another unit and marginal cost is its additional cost. Extra output adds to profit while marginal revenue exceeds marginal cost. A profit maximum can occur where they are equal with marginal cost crossing marginal revenue from below; equality alone is not sufficient evidence of a maximum. For a discrete table, compare total profit directly rather than inventing an exact equality absent from the data. Topic 5.3 develops the cost and revenue measures.

Profit is a goal, but a supplied firm may also value survival, service or stable employment. An evaluation should apply its stated objective rather than assume maximum current profit settles every decision. Reliability and future demand can also affect which option really delivers the highest attainable profit over the relevant period.
""")
t.block('02','definition','Production includes useful services',"""
Production is the creation of goods and services to satisfy wants. A potter making a bowl produces a good; a repairer restoring a bicycle provides a service. Both use resources to generate something that meets a want. Restricting production to factory manufacture would omit agriculture and services, even though those activities contribute output.

A private firm acts as a producer when it organises resources to provide such output. It acts as an employer when it hires workers to help. These roles are connected but distinct: a self-employed person can produce alone, while an expanding firm may recruit workers. Production is not synonymous with employment, and buying an existing asset is not itself evidence that the buyer has created new output.

At Kalahari Clay Works, clay, worker time, a kiln and the owner’s organisation combine to make bowls. Repairing the kiln is a service input that supports later bowl production. Naming both the output and the resources used makes the description precise. The physical transformation of clay is one example of production, not a requirement that every productive activity transform material into a new object.
""")
t.block('03','plain_explanation','Demand for resources follows the need for output',"""
Factors of production are demanded because of their contribution to output. If customers want more bowls at profitable prices, the pottery may demand more labour, clay and kiln time. This is derived demand: it comes from demand for the final output rather than workers or equipment being acquired simply for their own sake. The strength of the effect depends on how much additional production is needed and how it can be achieved.

Factor prices and productivity influence the chosen method. A machine providing the same usable output with lower relevant costs may become attractive as wages rise. However, a cheaper machine is not a useful substitute if it cannot make the required product. Some factors complement each other: more machinery can increase demand for skilled maintenance labour. Distinguish the tasks replaced from the tasks newly required.

The time available matters. In the short run, existing premises or equipment may constrain expansion, so the firm cannot immediately change every input. Training or installing machinery can take time. Stronger product demand may initially lead to overtime rather than a new production line. Explain the supplied conditions and available alternatives instead of assuming every increase in sales demand creates the same number or type of jobs.
""")
t.block('04','comparison','Substituting capital for labour',"""
Substitution changes the factor combination used to produce output. A pottery may replace some manual shaping tasks with a machine while retaining skilled workers for design, operation and checking. Capital then substitutes for particular labour tasks and complements the workers who operate or maintain it. The effect on total employment depends on these changes and the scale of production, not simply the presence of a machine.

Use invented weekly methods producing and selling the same 100 acceptable pieces. The labour-intensive method has total relevant cost N$7,000. A machine-assisted method has total relevant cost N$6,200, including operation, training and maintenance for the comparison period. Both earn N$9,000 revenue. Profits are N$2,000 and N$2,800. The machine-assisted method improves profit by N$800 under those conditions. The comparison works because output, quality, sales and the cost coverage are stated.

Weak demand can change the conclusion. If a machine’s large fixed commitment remains while sales are low, its cost per sold unit may be unattractive. Unreliable electricity, unavailable maintenance or a need for customised manual work can reduce feasibility. Conversely, strong sustained demand can allow equipment to be used productively. Evaluate the actual total cost and revenue rather than calling capital automatically cheaper.

Before recommending substitution, test whether the output can be sold, the quality maintained and the required skills secured. If a listed cost is only a wage saving, subtracting it does not establish a net profit gain until machine costs are included. The best method is conditional on the firm’s objective and the relevant period; a method that gives more profit than before still needs comparison with the other feasible methods before it is called profit maximising.

As a contrasting invented case, weekly revenue under weak demand is N$3,000 with either method. Total relevant cost is N$2,500 for the labour method and N$3,200 for the machine method. Labour now yields N$500 profit while machine assistance produces a N$200 loss. Choosing labour is consistent with the profit goal under these new conditions. The previous N$800 machine advantage cannot be carried into this case because utilisation and full costs have changed. Nor does this comparison establish that labour is preferable at every output. It demonstrates why the decision requires matched data for the particular demand conditions being evaluated.
""")
def s(sub):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['subtype']==sub)
t.card(s('EVAL'),'01','Evaluate whether the highest-output option maximises profit using invented weekly output/revenue/total-cost options: 10 batches/N$1,000/N$800; 20/N$1,800/N$1,300; 30/N$2,400/N$2,000. All output sells and all relevant costs are included.','The 30-batch option has more revenue, but profit is N$400. The 20-batch option earns N$500 against N$200 at 10 batches, so it maximises profit among the listed feasible choices. More output is beneficial only while the added revenue exceeds added cost; the table does not establish an unlisted global maximum.',['Argument for higher output (1): more revenue. Argument against (2): extra cost matters (1), N$400 below N$500 (1). Condition (1): supplied feasible choices and full costs. Judgement (2): choose 20 (1), greatest listed profit N$500 (1). Limitation (1): no unlisted choice assessed. Total 7.'])
t.card(s('MISCON'),'01','Explain the error in claiming profit has been maximised merely because it rose from N$200 to N$400 while another feasible choice yields N$500.','Profit has increased but is not the highest feasible profit. The N$500 alternative exceeds N$400, so the improvement does not establish maximisation.',['Confusion (1): improvement mistaken for maximum. Boundary/test (1): compare with feasible N$500 alternative.'])
t.card(s('DEF'),'02','Define production.','The creation of goods and services to satisfy wants.',['Key term/meaning (1): creation of goods and services satisfying wants.'])
t.card(s('FEATURE'),'02','Identify one goods-producing activity and one service-producing activity using original examples.','Making clay bowls produces goods; repairing bicycles provides a service.',['Feature/example (1): making a tangible good. Distinct characteristic/example (1): providing a service.'])
slots=t.wo['item_slots']
def x(local,sub):return next(i for i in slots if i['objective_id']==t.oid(local) and i['subtype']==sub)
t.card(x('03','MECH'),'03','Explain how sustained stronger demand for bowls can increase the pottery’s demand for labour when extra workers are needed to produce additional saleable output.','More orders create a reason to expand bowl output. Additional workers contribute to that output, so the firm’s derived demand for labour rises under the stated production condition. The final-product demand drives the factor demand.',['Starting change (1): stronger bowl demand. Mechanism (2): more saleable output needed (1), workers contribute to expansion (1). Consequence (1): derived labour demand rises under stated condition.'])
t.card(x('03','APP'),'03','Explain why buying another machine may increase demand for maintenance skills even if it replaces manual shaping tasks.','The machine substitutes for shaping work but requires maintenance. Capital and maintenance labour are complements, so demand for that skill may rise despite reduced demand for another task.',['Context (1): different shaping and maintenance tasks. Reason/consequence (1): machine complements maintenance skill.'])
t.card(x('04','MECH'),'04','Explain the profit effect of replacing a labour-intensive method costing N$7,000 with a machine-assisted method costing N$6,200, each producing the same saleable quality and N$9,000 revenue; all relevant costs are included.','The factor combination changes without changing revenue. Total cost falls N$800, raising profit from N$2,000 to N$2,800. The gain depends on the stated equal output, quality, sales and comprehensive cost conditions.',['Starting change (1): factor substitution. Mechanism (2): cost falls N$800 (1), revenue unchanged (1). Consequence (1): profit rises N$800 with full costs included.'])
t.card(x('04','APP'),'04','Explain why a wage saving alone is insufficient evidence that substituting a machine raises profit.','Machine operation, maintenance, training and other relevant costs may offset the wage saving. Compare total costs and any revenue change before concluding profit rises.',['Context (1): wage saving is partial. Reason/conclusion (1): full costs and revenue determine net gain.'])
schedule='Invented weekly choices with all output sold and all relevant costs included: 10 batches revenue N$1,000/cost N$800; 20 batches N$1,800/N$1,300; 30 batches N$2,400/N$2,000.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][0],'02 03 04 01','Define production (2). Identify two determinants of factor demand (2). Explain a reason to substitute capital for labour when the same saleable output can be produced at lower total relevant cost (2). '+schedule+' Identify the profit-maximising output and its profit among the choices (2).','Production creates goods and services to satisfy wants. Output demand and factor productivity influence factor demand. Lower total cost at unchanged revenue raises profit and can motivate capital substitution. Profits are N$200, N$500 and N$400; 20 batches gives the greatest listed profit of N$500.',['Meaning (2): goods/services created (1), satisfy wants (1). Separate points (2): output demand (1), factor productivity (1). Explanation (2): cost reduction with same output (1), profit increase at unchanged revenue (1). Identification (2): 20 batches (1), N$500 (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
base='Invented pottery methods for identical quality and 100 pieces sold weekly: labour-intensive total relevant cost N$7,000; machine-assisted total relevant cost N$6,200 including operation, maintenance and training. Each earns revenue N$9,000. The machine replaces some shaping tasks but requires skilled operation. Buyers demand the 100 pieces and required operators are available. No other feasible method is described.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][1],'01 02 03 04',base+' Describe production, the profit goal and the difference between revenue and profit using this case (6). Explain how product demand and factor substitution affect the choice and factor demand (7). Discuss whether the machine method can be called profit maximising (7).','The pottery creates bowls satisfying wants. Profit maximisation seeks the greatest feasible revenue-cost difference; N$9,000 is revenue, with profits N$2,000 and N$2,800. Buyers support output and derived factor demand. Capital replaces shaping tasks, complements operation skills and lowers cost N$800 at unchanged revenue. The machine method is best of the two described, subject to quality, cost coverage and saleability. It cannot be established as the greatest of all feasible methods when alternatives are unspecified.',['Data description (6): production creates output (1), bowls satisfy wants (1); maximum profit goal (1), greatest feasible difference (1); revenue N$9,000 (1), profit after cost (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): buyers demand output (1), derived factor demand (1), substitution of shaping capital (1), complementary operators (1), total cost N$800 lower (1), revenue unchanged (1), profit N$800 higher (1). Both sides/judgement (7): machine N$2,800 (1), exceeds labour N$2,000 (1); required operators/quality condition (1), sales/full cost condition (1); other methods unassessed (1); judgement best listed (1), wider maximum not established (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
cases='Invented same-quality methods for a private pottery. Case 1: strong confirmed orders permit full utilisation; total weekly revenue N$9,000 under either method, full relevant cost N$7,000 labour-intensive or N$6,200 machine-assisted. Required machine skills and reliable power are available. Case 2: weak orders leave machinery idle; total weekly revenue is N$3,000 under either method and full relevant cost is N$2,500 labour-intensive or N$3,200 machine-assisted. The machine replaces some manual tasks in both cases; these are the two feasible methods.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][2],'01 04',cases+' Evaluate capital substitution for profit maximisation in Case 1 (7). Evaluate it in Case 2 (7).','Case 1: substitution lowers full cost N$800, increasing profit from N$2,000 to N$2,800. The recommendation depends on confirmed sales, reliability, skills and equal quality, so machine assistance is best of the two. Case 2: idle machinery carries costs without corresponding sales; labour earns N$500 while machine assistance loses N$200. Retain the labour method under these demand conditions; stronger orders or different full costs could change the result.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for N$800 cost saving (1), profit N$2,800 (1); argument against reliance on sales (1), operational feasibility (1); condition confirmed demand/skills/power (1); judgement substitute (1), highest of feasible options (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument against idle capital cost (1), loss N$200 (1); argument for possible machine gain if demand changed (1), substitution can lower cost at utilisation (1); condition weak current orders (1); judgement labour (1), profit N$500 exceeds alternative (1). Total 14.'],[cases])
assert [r-c for r,c in [(1000,800),(1800,1300),(2400,2000)]]==[200,500,400]
